#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502698 :question/problem "The inequality $- \\frac{2}{5} \\le x \\le \\frac{1}{3}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $- \\frac{2}{5}$ and $\\frac{1}{3}$ *are* included.\n\n$$\n- \\frac{2}{5}\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, \\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}- \\frac{2}{5},\\frac{1}{3}{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [- \\frac{2}{5},\\frac{1}{3}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502699 :question/problem "The inequality $- 2 \\sqrt{2} \\le x \\le - \\sqrt{3}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $- 2 \\sqrt{2}$ and $- \\sqrt{3}$ *are* included.\n\n$$\n- 2 \\sqrt{2}\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, - \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}- 2 \\sqrt{2},- \\sqrt{3}{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [- 2 \\sqrt{2},- \\sqrt{3}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502701 :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x ∈ [-  \\frac{9}{2}, - \\frac{2}{3}]$?" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only brackets. So, the endpoints are *included*.\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}- \\frac{9}{2}, - \\frac{2}{3}{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{9}{2} \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\le}}\\,\\, x \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\le}}\\,\\, - \\frac{2}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502713 :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x\\in [-11,3]$?" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $(,)$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only brackets. So, the endpoints *are* included. \n\n$$\nx\\in [-11,3]\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-11\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,x\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502715 :question/problem "The inequality $-4\\le x\\le -2$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-4$ and $-2$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n-4\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,x\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-4,-2]\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [-4,-2].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502718 :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x ∈ [- 11, 8]$?" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only brackets. So, the endpoints are *included*.\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-11, 8{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-11 \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\le}}\\,\\, x \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\le}}\\,\\, 8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502734 :question/problem "The inequality $-11<x<-3$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"less than\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-11$ and $-3$ are *not* included. \n\n$$\n-11\\,\\,<\\,\\,x\\,\\,<\\,\\,-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n(-11,-3)\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (-11,-3).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502744 :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x ∈ (10, 11)$?" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only parentheses. So, the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}10, 11{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n10 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<}}\\,\\, x \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<}}\\,\\, 11\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502749 :question/problem "The inequality $-\\sqrt{7}<x<\\sqrt{7}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"less than\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-\\sqrt{7}$ and $\\sqrt{7}$ are *not* included. \n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\,<\\,\\,x\\,\\,<\\,\\,\\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n(-\\sqrt{7},\\sqrt{7})\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\sqrt{7},\\sqrt{7}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502760 :question/problem "![](images/7c/7cea6742f633db7d6366c4d6875bb3c544eb522e986448e0c18e052091d5a844.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-1,2{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502762 :question/problem "![](images/ed/eda4e34daa187c5e171ab3444ab0ecb99e3c30d52e26d7cb8ee060990249c521.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, 0\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}-\\frac{5}{2},0{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502764 :question/problem "![](images/24/245df794524514e9f8b9c7c0edca9992bb95e6ae2cf4d025e09dc827e262cd65.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-1\\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, 3\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}-1,3{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502775 :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca28fe03b54c1b9d6a5a2d50fc7afb70c8a4b5042e2c77608a2e83740c6cdcb5.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-3\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, -2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-3,-2{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502776 :question/problem "![](images/da/da7c02082d1179b4f90025f1a25f9eb039647068743414b4502a9790df867b7b.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, \\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-1,\\frac{3}{2}{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502789 :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b79233c4c56df37837eeeb780249c01667857a5236716892ea0356b0131515f.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-4\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 0\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-4,0{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502791 :question/problem "![](images/06/06fcafc739be96ac3a5c1b1a7dd200ee3465d9cc3d7df3f75c13f996990afc21.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-3\\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, 1\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}-3,1{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502793 :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d59de12d2015b2f2901bbb217eb8f57d24219c990b51adbe68ef377e96714e0.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, -\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}-\\frac{7}{2},-\\frac{5}{2}{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502804 :question/problem "![](images/31/31c04f13fed156a189ed06db2a7130512820944ae67ed6592b70e62dae56623c.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 3\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}1,3{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502805 :question/problem "![](images/9c/9cbc74f8d53deb119497da850bed43dd643ee22753a43918b1017335e692a18a.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n0\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}0,2{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502808 :question/problem "![](images/b6/b6081782588b4fbb26ea28685b10f69110ddd155cc31b9f36ed27aac9e2f9cab.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-2\\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, 1\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}-2,1{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502811 :question/problem "![](images/db/db9a24ca46cced61a351578eff6c6c2c849cdcdb484ee74baa65e3a9bed20b02.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, 2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}-\\frac{3}{2},2{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502819 :question/problem "![](images/49/49c660f1ed7cd87ca6c6dcd85df0e58c8de7d3fcd797830533728b54d9f902df.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-4\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, -\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-4,-\\frac{3}{2}{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502843 :question/problem "![](images/34/3456fba09f4b2018bc8b5508d3df08253ed8ff8176ad1ca6ae1f5c18a2fd538f.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}1,2{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502863 :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{13}}}$. Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Note that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{13}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{13/7}}={x}^{-13/7}.\n$$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{13}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{13}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-13/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-13/7+1}}{-\\frac{13}{7}+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{-6/7}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{6{x}^{6/7}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502872 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-7/9}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{7}{9}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-7/9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-7/9+1}}{(-\\frac{7}{9}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2/9}}{\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2/9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502876 :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{5}}}$. Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Note that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{5}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/3}}={x}^{-5/3}.\n$$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{5}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-5/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/3+1}}{-\\frac{5}{3}+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2/3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502879 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-4/7}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{4}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-4/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/7+1}}{(-\\frac{4}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/7}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{7}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502887 :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{10}}}$. Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Note that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{10}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{10/3}}={x}^{-10/3}.\n$$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{10}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{10}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-10/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-10/3+1}}{-\\frac{10}{3}+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{7}{x}^{-7/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{7{x}^{7/3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502891 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-1/4}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/4+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/4}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3/4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502900 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/4}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/4}}={x}^{-5/4}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{5}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-5/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/4+1}}{(-\\frac{5}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/4}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-4{x}^{-1/4}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{1/4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502914 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-2/7}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{2}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-2/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-2/7+1}}{(-\\frac{2}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/7}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{7}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}{x}^{5/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502920 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-1/5}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/5+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/5}}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{4/5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502921 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{8/5}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{8/5}}={x}^{-8/5}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{8}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-8/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-8/5+1}}{(-\\frac{8}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/5}}{\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{-3/5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3{x}^{3/5}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502927 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6/5}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{6/5}}={x}^{-6/5}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{6}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-6/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-6/5+1}}{(-\\frac{6}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/5}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-5{x}^{-1/5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{{x}^{1/5}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502931 :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[6]{{x}^{13}}}$. Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Note that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[6]{{x}^{13}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{13/6}}={x}^{-13/6}.\n$$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{13}{6}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[6]{{x}^{13}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-13/6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-13/6+1}}{-\\frac{13}{6}+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{7}{x}^{-7/6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{7{x}^{7/6}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502938 :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}?$ Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Note that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}={x}^{-6}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-6$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-6+1}}{-6+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-5}}{5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5{x}^{5}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502948 :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[6]{{x}^{7}}}$. Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Note that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[6]{{x}^{7}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{7/6}}={x}^{-7/6}.\n$$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{7}{6}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[6]{{x}^{7}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-7/6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-7/6+1}}{-\\frac{7}{6}+1}+C \\\\  & =-6{x}^{-1/6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{{x}^{1/6}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502954 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{9/5}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{9/5}}={x}^{-9/5}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{9}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-9/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-9/5+1}}{(-\\frac{9}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/5}}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{-4/5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4{x}^{4/5}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503663 :question/problem "![](images/e7/e74f96531b57a877b043ac4302adae6beef90dc24d64efc8e1a24b563e4738b7.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the ellipse shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our ellipse is centered at $(-1,2)$ and has semiaxes $4$ and $2,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(x+1)}{16}+\\frac{2(y-2)}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+1}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+1}{4(y-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503744 :question/problem "The slope of the line is $m\\,=\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Note that the line passes through the points $(0,960)$ and $(16,0).$ So, we let $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,960)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(16,0),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-960}{16-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-960}{16} \\\\  & =-60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is $m=-60.$"}]}
